Added the client-credentials grant and the Bearer plugin — what makes an issued token actually authenticate a request. `@@oauth-token` now serves both grants, and they fail on opposite principles: the authorization-code path answers every refusal with the same sentence because the caller may be guessing, while client credentials name the failure, because by then the caller has already proved it holds the client secret and every remaining problem is a registration mistake somebody has to be able to read. A client-credentials token acts as a **service user** nominated on the registration, not as the client id: a `sub` naming something Plone has never heard of would authenticate a principal no roles plugin can answer for, so the grant is refused outright when the service user is unset or does not exist, and authorization stays where an operator can see it. Public clients cannot use the grant at all — RFC 6749 §4.4 requires client authentication, and unlike the code flow there is no PKCE exchange standing in for it. The server's PAS plugin, until now only a persistent home for authorization codes, activates `IExtractionPlugin` and `IAuthenticationPlugin`: extraction is one header read and a prefix test, so an ordinary request pays nothing, and the signature check happens only for a request that actually presented a token. It deliberately does not challenge — a failed token falls through to whatever the site already does, rather than an add-on deciding the site is an API. Beyond signature and expiry, the audience is looked up in the client registry and the subject in the user folder on every request: with no denylist (D3), disabling or unregistering a client is the only revocation this server has, and a token must not outlive the user it was minted for. @ericof
